1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,200
They're the tools that make work fun.

2
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:10,980
Manufactured by household names and Hollywood celebrities.

3
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:17,240
Easier to use and more powerful than ever.

4
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:20,900
But they weren't always like this.

5
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,540
Now, Power Tools on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,420
Hi, I'm Ron Hazleton. Welcome to the Tool Bench.

7
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:46,220
You know, my dad built his own house when he was only 19 years old,

8
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,900
and that was long before you could run out to the local mega home center to pick up a good power tool.

9
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:57,260
I gained a real respect for hand tools from my dad, but I don't think I'd ever want to give up my power tools.

10
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:01,620
I've gotten so used to them, it's hard to imagine a time when they weren't around.

11
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:06,800
But in the long history of tools, power, well, that's just the tip of the iceberg.

12
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:10,940
Beginning with primitive tools made from rocks,

13
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:16,900
human civilization could be measured by how we make, use, and improve tools.

14
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,980
From stone to copper, and later iron and steel,

15
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:25,120
we've been making tools for at least two and a half million years.

16
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:37,140
At the very end of this spectrum, about .0004% of the tool timeline are power tools.

17
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:48,060
The ability to motorize and electrify tools made it possible to build houses, skyscrapers,

18
00:01:48,061 --> 00:01:53,520
and entire communities with unprecedented speed and efficiency.

19
00:01:53,521 --> 00:01:58,720
They've also made using tools just a little more exciting.

20
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:02,640
I think the mystique is all about power and speed.

21
00:02:02,641 --> 00:02:07,140
And I don't know that males are more interested in power and speed than females,

22
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:11,840
but I think everybody is interested in getting a job done more quickly and more efficiently.

23
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:15,540
Today, the power tool industry is booming,

24
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:21,480
thanks to the steady introduction of tools that are lighter, cordless, and more powerful than ever,

25
00:02:21,481 --> 00:02:24,200
which for many is the best part of all.

26
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:29,840
There's the guy I know that doesn't say more horsepower would be better,

27
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:31,580
bigger, badder lawnmower,

28
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:35,020
and most of the guys I know will modify just about anything.

29
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:39,300
Lawnmower, you know, if you take the Venturi out of that carburetor,

30
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:40,540
you can get a little bit more horsepower.

31
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:42,580
Is there any reason to get more horsepower out of a lawnmower?

32
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:43,360
I don't think so.

33
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:45,700
But always more horsepower is better.

34
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:50,320
Powered by stronger batteries and more efficient motors,

35
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:56,920
Today's portable cordless power tools can perform just as effectively as their cord-bound predecessors.

36
00:02:57,620 --> 00:03:00,140
And batteries are not the only energy source.

37
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,740
Some tools are powered by compressed air.

38
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:10,100
The Stanley Bostitch Company is most commonly associated with desktop staplers.

39
00:03:10,101 --> 00:03:18,240
But at their East Greenwich, Rhode Island plant, they produce some of the best-selling pneumatic nail and staple guns on the market today.

40
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:32,800
This roofing stapler, powered by a 90 pounds per square inch burst of compressed air, can drive a roll of staples in seconds.

41
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:37,940
And it's designed to never jam, no matter how inept the user.

42
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:44,000
A different type of nail gun is manufactured at the Porter Cable Plant in Jackson, Tennessee.

43
00:03:44,001 --> 00:03:53,000
The BAMR is powered not by compressed air, but by compressed liquefied gas, also known as a fuel cell.

44
00:03:53,001 --> 00:03:59,000
The advantage of this energy source is that it doesn't have to be connected to an air compressor to work.

45
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Meanwhile, at the Ryobi plant in Pickens, South Carolina,

46
00:04:06,001 --> 00:04:15,000
A reciprocating saw that could tear through walls runs off liquid gasoline, just like a lawnmower.

47
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
But for the vast majority of power tool users, the preferred energy source is still the battery.

48
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Thanks to the power of today's batteries, circular saws can cut through wood like a knife through butter.

49
00:04:32,001 --> 00:04:32,701
butter.

50
00:04:32,702 --> 00:04:37,680
Drill bits can be powered to bore holes just as fast as you can pull the trigger.

51
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:46,151
But behind the story of circular saws and drill bits are two now forgotten names.

52
00:04:46,152 --> 00:04:47,260
They made their

53
00:04:47,261 --> 00:04:51,458
marks on the tool industry before we even had electricity.

54
00:04:51,459 --> 00:04:54,280
One was a woman, the other a blacksmith

55
00:04:54,281 --> 00:04:58,660
with a fondness for whiskey, and their influence is still felt today.

56
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:07,020
In the 1800s, the Shakers, a religious community that had flourished in the eastern United States,

57
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:11,600
were well known for their exceptional furniture and woodworking skills.

58
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:17,920
But it was a Shaker sister named Tabitha Babbitt who in 1810 got a great idea.

59
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:21,380
It came to her while working at her spinning wheel.

60
00:05:22,180 --> 00:05:32,160
She worked around a lot of men who toiled day in and day out sawing logs the way that they did it for a century,

61
00:05:32,340 --> 00:05:35,520
which was basically they would take a log and position it over a pit.

62
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:43,640
And one man was in the pit and one man was above, and they took a large handsaw and simply sawed through the log.

63
00:05:43,641 --> 00:05:49,540
And, of course, she saw that that was incredibly labor-intensive and very hard work.

64
00:05:49,541 --> 00:05:54,760
and so she came up with the idea of let's take that straight blade, put it on a wheel.

65
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:02,840
It occurred to Babbitt that a circular revolving blade could cut wood more efficiently than hand saws.

66
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:11,240
Her idea was adopted by shaker woodworkers who powered the circular saws by hand cranks and later by water wheels.

67
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:16,040
So think of Tabitha the next time you pick up that circular saw blade,

68
00:06:16,041 --> 00:06:19,740
the one you probably assumed was invented by a man.

69
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:25,900
The next time you change a drill bit, you might think of this Ohio blacksmith.

70
00:06:26,780 --> 00:06:33,140
In 1884, William Mac Dimmitt took out a patent on a drill bit that he forged out of steel.

71
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:38,380
Prior to Dimmitt, drill bits were either similar to straight, tapered files,

72
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:40,620
or they had a screw-like twist.

73
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:47,920
What made Demet's unique was that it had a solid stem running through the middle of a screw-like twist.

74
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:53,100
Demet probably didn't realize that he'd created one of the best bits ever made.

75
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:57,980
But today, you'll never find William Demet's name in a hardware store.

76
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:00,700
But you will find his drill bit.

77
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:05,160
Here's my favorite drill bit.

78
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:10,240
This is an Erwin Auger, which sounds like a cartoon character name, but it's not.

79
00:07:10,241 --> 00:07:13,120
It's a guy named Charles Irwin who was a pharmacist in Ohio.

80
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:16,220
Now, how does a pharmacist get a drill bit?

81
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:20,840
Well, this is back in the 1880s when drugstores actually sold alcoholic beverages.

82
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:28,580
One of his customers was William Demet, who loved to come in every day and consume some of these alcoholic beverages,

83
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:31,060
ran up a bill and then couldn't pay it.

84
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,040
He had invented this.

85
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:35,360
William Demet had invented this drill bit.

86
00:07:35,361 --> 00:07:41,620
So he traded the patent on this to Charles Irwin for his back bill of alcohol.

87
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:49,980
William Dimmett sold his patent to Charles Irwin for the dismissal of his $20 back bill in a bottle of whiskey.

88
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:54,440
Irwin immediately formed a company to manufacture the drill bit.

89
00:07:54,441 --> 00:07:57,600
Still manufactured today

90
00:07:57,601 --> 00:07:59,760
About 100 million

91
00:07:59,761 --> 00:08:01,280
Erwin Auger drill bits

92
00:08:01,281 --> 00:08:02,820
Have been sold since the day

93
00:08:02,821 --> 00:08:05,140
William Dimmitt traded his patent

94
00:08:05,141 --> 00:08:06,820
For a whiskey debt

95
00:08:06,821 --> 00:08:11,240
The contributions of Tabitha Babbitt

96
00:08:11,241 --> 00:08:12,780
And William Dimmitt aside

97
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:15,320
It wasn't until 1895

98
00:08:15,321 --> 00:08:17,320
That the world would see the first

99
00:08:17,321 --> 00:08:19,620
Portable electric power tool

100
00:08:19,621 --> 00:08:23,940
It came a full 16 years

101
00:08:23,941 --> 00:08:26,340
after Thomas Edison harnessed electricity

102
00:08:26,341 --> 00:08:28,960
to power the incandescent electric lamp.

103
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:31,800
The tool was a drill

104
00:08:31,801 --> 00:08:35,720
invented by the German engineering firm of C&E Fein.

105
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:38,540
It weighed 16 1⁄2 pounds

106
00:08:38,541 --> 00:08:41,560
and was powered not only by its DC motor,

107
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:43,540
but by the weight of the user.

108
00:08:44,260 --> 00:08:46,300
It would take about another 20 years

109
00:08:46,301 --> 00:08:50,140
before two men would significantly improve the electric drill

110
00:08:50,141 --> 00:08:53,340
and become household names as a result.

111
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:56,000
The year was 1910.

112
00:08:56,460 --> 00:09:00,820
A tool and die worker named Duncan Black sold his car for $600.

113
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:07,400
He used the money to open a machine shop in Baltimore with his friend Alonzo Decker.

114
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:13,040
Thoughts of designing a new type of drill had intrigued them, but they weren't sure how to do it.

115
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:18,700
Then, in 1914, an idea seemed to consume them both at the same moment.

116
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:21,600
They were both in my father's kitchen one Saturday,

117
00:09:21,601 --> 00:09:24,220
and they were sitting around the table

118
00:09:24,221 --> 00:09:27,460
trying to get a bright idea

119
00:09:27,461 --> 00:09:29,160
of how they could do this.

120
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:31,880
And Father had a Colt automatic

121
00:09:31,881 --> 00:09:33,120
laying on the kitchen table.

122
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:36,860
And as they were looking at the sky

123
00:09:36,861 --> 00:09:38,480
or the stars or whatever,

124
00:09:39,380 --> 00:09:40,860
each one of them happened to look

125
00:09:40,861 --> 00:09:42,080
at that Colt automatic.

126
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:44,680
And so my mother tells me

127
00:09:44,681 --> 00:09:46,560
that the two of them said,

128
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:50,240
that's it, at the same time.

129
00:09:50,241 --> 00:09:57,480
The epiphany Black & Decker had was to adapt the design of the Colt 45 to the electric drill.

130
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:03,220
In 1916, they began marketing the first pistol grip trigger switch drills.

131
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:10,100
This is one of the first Black & Decker drills housed at the company headquarters in Townsend, Maryland.

132
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:16,060
Black & Decker's design resulted in a lighter drill that could be handled by one man.

133
00:10:16,061 --> 00:10:20,880
The motor was smaller and more powerful than those used on the German drills.

134
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:25,400
Suddenly, the power tool industry was off and running,

135
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:30,500
and right on the heels of Black & Decker would come other familiar names.

136
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:37,740
In 1969, the Erwin Augerbit plant came to a screeching halt

137
00:10:37,741 --> 00:10:41,220
when a runaway train crashed through the factory wall.

138
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:43,860
Remarkably, no one was injured.

139
00:10:43,861 --> 00:10:47,180
Power tools will return on Modern Marbles.

140
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:53,680
Today, almost everyone has used a power drill.

141
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:59,260
But one of the oldest power tools might be the least familiar to the novice do-it-yourselfer.

142
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:01,740
This is a router.

143
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:09,940
These small, round tools are used to spin a variety of bits to cut grooves into wood pieces like doors or cabinets.

144
00:11:09,941 --> 00:11:14,800
The router was invented three years after Black & Decker's pistol grip drill.

145
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:21,480
The year was 1919, and the inventor was a pattern maker named R.L. Carter.

146
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:27,580
This now made it possible for people to put molded profiles on the edges of work pieces,

147
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:32,260
whereas before they had to have very expensive, very elaborate hand planes,

148
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:36,340
which were difficult to sharpen, difficult to operate,

149
00:11:36,341 --> 00:11:40,720
and now you had one electric tool that could do it and almost anybody could operate it.

150
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:49,720
Carter came up with the idea for the router while reworking an electric barber's clipper in his garage.

151
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:55,200
In the years to follow, there would be a steady stream of power tool innovations.

152
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:03,000
Herbert Tauts entered the field in 1923 from his Milwaukee, Wisconsin garage.

153
00:12:03,700 --> 00:12:08,160
There he invented the earliest version of this tool, the scroll saw.

154
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:13,380
The scroll saw has a cutting blade that reciprocates in an up-and-down fashion.

155
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:17,360
Tots called his model the American Boy Scroll Saw.

156
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:21,040
The user cranked a handle to put the saw in motion.

157
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:29,800
But Tots also sold a scroll saw with a 1 20th horsepower motor in place of the crank.

158
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:35,080
The manual saw sold for $6 and was popular with hobbyists,

159
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:40,520
while the motorized version was sold to professionals for four times as much.

160
00:12:42,460 --> 00:12:44,940
Tots' company no longer operates out of a garage.

161
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:50,100
Today, it's the Delta Corporation, a leader in the industry.

162
00:12:50,660 --> 00:12:56,711
In 1918, automobile maker Henry Ford challenged a young tool manufacturer, A.H.

163
00:12:56,712 --> 00:12:57,412
Peterson,

164
00:12:57,413 --> 00:13:02,800
to produce a smaller, lighter electric drill for long days on the assembly line.

165
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:09,980
Peterson delighted Ford with a five-pound electric drill,

166
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:13,560
capable of handling the heavy demands of the car industry.

167
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:18,220
It was also the first drill that could be operated with only one hand.

168
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:21,420
Peterson called it the hole shooter.

169
00:13:21,421 --> 00:13:30,135
In 1924, facing financial difficulties caused by a fire at his plant, Peterson sold the hole shooter to A.F.

170
00:13:30,136 --> 00:13:35,300
Siebert, who used the drill to begin the Milwaukee Tool Company.

171
00:13:36,160 --> 00:13:40,760
A pioneer in the power saw industry got its start in the 20s as well.

172
00:13:41,560 --> 00:13:49,280
That's when an inventor from Louisiana, Edmund Michel, stood in a sugar cane field and was appalled by what he witnessed.

173
00:13:49,281 --> 00:13:54,120
Cutting sugar cane was a brutally difficult job

174
00:13:54,121 --> 00:13:57,260
Field hands withering in the hot sun

175
00:13:57,261 --> 00:14:01,380
Would swing machetes over and over again to cut the thick cane

176
00:14:01,381 --> 00:14:07,420
Michel set out to ease the grueling work by attempting to motorize a machete

177
00:14:07,421 --> 00:14:13,780
But like Tabitha Babbitt, he eventually concluded that a round blade would work even better

178
00:14:13,781 --> 00:14:18,160
Using the motor from a malted milk mixer to power the blade

179
00:14:18,161 --> 00:14:22,900
his handheld circular saw barely cut through a cigar box.

180
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:25,920
But Michel would improve upon his invention.

181
00:14:26,620 --> 00:14:33,140
In 1924, he began selling his 10-pound skill saw for an astronomical $160.

182
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:37,620
In today's economy, that would be more than $1,500.

183
00:14:38,620 --> 00:14:42,160
And even at that price, it had serious limitations.

184
00:14:42,161 --> 00:14:49,280
In 1926, a young inventor was inspired by Edmund Michel's success with the skill saw.

185
00:14:50,180 --> 00:14:55,040
At the time, Art Emmons was working for the Porter Cable Company in Syracuse, New York.

186
00:14:55,760 --> 00:15:01,800
Named after the company's two founders, Porter Cable started in 1906 making pencil sharpeners.

187
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:07,500
And of course, like other early tool manufacturers, they made them out of a garage.

188
00:15:07,501 --> 00:15:12,260
But by the 20s, Art Emmons believed the promise of power tools was great.

189
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:17,580
Emmons envisioned a way to solve the labor-intensive jobs of sanding and finishing.

190
00:15:18,640 --> 00:15:23,020
Up until that time, all sanding had to take place on large stationary machines.

191
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:28,240
So he felt that if you could design a product that instead of either sanding by hand

192
00:15:28,241 --> 00:15:31,960
or having to pick something large up and carry it to a machine to be sanded,

193
00:15:32,180 --> 00:15:33,940
it would greatly improve productivity.

194
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:41,520
In 1926, Emmons invented the first portable belt sander.

195
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:47,980
Weighing less than 15 pounds, it enabled workers to carry the tool to the work instead of vice versa.

196
00:15:49,060 --> 00:15:51,480
Because of this, Emmons called it the takeabout.

197
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:57,280
It would garner the young inventor the first of more than 50 patents for tools and parts.

198
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:03,400
Emmons' takeabout would be Porter Cable's best-selling tool for decades to come.

199
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:08,080
He designed it with features that would take competitors years to equal.

200
00:16:09,020 --> 00:16:13,220
He installed a dust collection system and positioned the motor in such a way

201
00:16:13,221 --> 00:16:16,900
that the takeabout could even sand flush up against baseboards.

202
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:22,800
Thanks to the innovations of the 1920s, the power tool industry was growing,

203
00:16:23,380 --> 00:16:27,660
even in a world where whole communities were still without electricity.

204
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:33,320
But just as this new industry was gaining momentum, turmoil was about to strike.

205
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:35,400
and it would strike hard.

206
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:42,260
It's estimated that there are 106,000 power tool accidents

207
00:16:42,261 --> 00:16:45,580
requiring medical attention in the United States each year.

208
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:49,460
The Tool Bench Power Tools will return.

209
00:16:57,160 --> 00:16:59,420
Now this is my idea of a power tool.

210
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:02,980
Today you can buy a tool like this from stores, catalogs,

211
00:17:02,981 --> 00:17:03,800
or internet services.

212
00:17:04,380 --> 00:17:07,520
And you can just as easily get any advice you need on how to use it.

213
00:17:08,200 --> 00:17:10,880
This being the information age, information is everywhere.

214
00:17:11,600 --> 00:17:15,000
But imagine being one of the first power tool salesmen

215
00:17:15,001 --> 00:17:18,140
at a time when a lot of people didn't even have electricity

216
00:17:18,141 --> 00:17:21,260
and your tool cost a month's salary.

217
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:23,080
Pretty tough sell.

218
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:28,660
When a power tool company offers a new product today,

219
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:31,840
it can show it off to millions of people at once.

220
00:17:32,420 --> 00:17:34,840
All it takes is one TV commercial.

221
00:17:35,700 --> 00:17:37,800
But it wasn't always this easy.

222
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:43,380
During the 1920s and 30s, when the power tool industry was in its infancy,

223
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:49,780
there was no television nor gigantic home centers with employees trained to use the tool.

224
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:53,940
So how did these fledgling power tool companies sell their product?

225
00:17:55,660 --> 00:17:59,020
They had salespeople who used to put these tools in the trunk of their car,

226
00:17:59,021 --> 00:18:02,320
and they'd drive around the streets of their community looking for job sites

227
00:18:02,321 --> 00:18:04,640
or where contractors or professionals were being used,

228
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,780
and they would go in and they would go ahead and show the features and the benefits of this product

229
00:18:08,781 --> 00:18:12,480
and convince the owner that he had to part with often months' wages,

230
00:18:12,580 --> 00:18:15,760
maybe even two months' wages to buy one of these newfangled electric tools,

231
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:17,660
but he could always sell the productivity.

232
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:23,180
As if it wasn't enough to convince someone to give up a month's salary for a tool,

233
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:27,500
these determined salesmen had an even bigger obstacle in their way.

234
00:18:28,460 --> 00:18:30,880
Many of the markets they went to didn't even have electricity,

235
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:35,220
so a lot of the salesmen, it said, would carry portable generators that were gas-driven

236
00:18:35,221 --> 00:18:39,500
so they could fire up the generator to show the particular individual or prospective buyer

237
00:18:39,501 --> 00:18:41,620
what this tool would do once they got electricity.

238
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,680
So you can imagine what a hard sell that was.

239
00:18:44,380 --> 00:18:48,480
When it needed it the most, the industry would receive an important boost

240
00:18:48,481 --> 00:18:51,880
in the form of instant mass marketing and credibility.

241
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:58,160
In 1928, Sears Roebuck began offering power tools in their popular mail order catalog.

242
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:04,300
It was a time when many Americans still lived in far-flung farming communities

243
00:19:04,301 --> 00:19:08,660
and did not have many choices or opportunities for buying new products.

244
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:14,460
But when the catalog arrived, it was an exciting chance to see how the world was changing

245
00:19:14,461 --> 00:19:17,100
from the swing on their own front porch.

246
00:19:17,780 --> 00:19:21,100
They would arrive and they would spend days, weeks, social hours,

247
00:19:21,220 --> 00:19:25,000
turning the pages of that catalog, looking at these new innovative products.

248
00:19:25,460 --> 00:19:29,420
And here you are in an agricultural community or in a farming community,

249
00:19:29,421 --> 00:19:33,360
and you see a drill or anything that could make your job easier.

250
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:36,980
Once electricity got there, it was like the things dreams are made of.

251
00:19:37,020 --> 00:19:38,680
You'd sit there and you'd think about it at night.

252
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:40,920
Because remember, there was no Monday night football.

253
00:19:41,480 --> 00:19:45,240
But in the blink of an eye, the power tool industry's fortunes,

254
00:19:45,241 --> 00:19:48,860
along with everyone else's, would take a change for the worse.

255
00:19:49,640 --> 00:19:54,260
On October 29, 1929, the bottom fell out of the stock market,

256
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:57,560
and neither Alonzo Decker's pistol grip drill

257
00:19:57,561 --> 00:20:02,580
nor Art Emmons' takeabout sander could do anything to fix this mess.

258
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:05,920
With the Depression, jobs dried up,

259
00:20:06,180 --> 00:20:09,360
and professionals could no longer afford expensive tools

260
00:20:09,361 --> 00:20:12,460
because they had no place to use them.

261
00:20:12,461 --> 00:20:18,880
The story at Black & Decker was the same as it was at other power tool companies.

262
00:20:19,520 --> 00:20:21,060
It was about layoffs.

263
00:20:21,800 --> 00:20:24,560
And the first to go was a recent college graduate,

264
00:20:25,040 --> 00:20:28,400
the son of the founding partner, Al Decker Jr.

265
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:32,700
My father called me in and said,

266
00:20:32,800 --> 00:20:35,940
Son, we're going to have to lay off an awful lot of people

267
00:20:35,941 --> 00:20:38,920
because business is really going to hell in a handbasket.

268
00:20:38,921 --> 00:20:42,740
and said, and I'm not going to lay off anybody else

269
00:20:42,741 --> 00:20:44,060
as long as you're here,

270
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:45,700
so you're going to have to be number one.

271
00:20:46,100 --> 00:20:47,680
I said, I understand, Father.

272
00:20:48,660 --> 00:20:49,360
Okay.

273
00:20:50,620 --> 00:20:53,560
In 1932, the newly elected president,

274
00:20:53,780 --> 00:20:54,920
Franklin D. Roosevelt,

275
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:57,440
was promising a new deal.

276
00:20:57,441 --> 00:20:59,040
Keep that faith high.

277
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:04,120
So shall we win through to a better day.

278
00:21:05,080 --> 00:21:07,560
And even though the country was still mired

279
00:21:07,561 --> 00:21:08,840
in financial turmoil,

280
00:21:08,920 --> 00:21:11,100
a sense of optimism arose.

281
00:21:11,680 --> 00:21:13,280
And most importantly, to builders,

282
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:16,780
the power tool industry offered a product that was essential

283
00:21:16,781 --> 00:21:19,340
to the promise of future growth.

284
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:23,560
Months after FDR's election,

285
00:21:23,860 --> 00:21:27,840
Sears offered their first catalog dedicated to power tools.

286
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:32,540
Craftsman would become a brand name for a variety of power tools

287
00:21:32,541 --> 00:21:36,380
made by different tool makers to meet the marketer's requirements.

288
00:21:37,380 --> 00:21:41,680
Even though power tools were still predominantly bought by professionals,

289
00:21:42,280 --> 00:21:45,040
they were sneaking their way into the consumer market.

290
00:21:46,100 --> 00:21:51,680
In 1932, A.J. Dremel, a Racine, Wisconsin engineer and inventor,

291
00:21:52,340 --> 00:21:55,980
launched a company from, you guessed it, his garage,

292
00:21:55,981 --> 00:22:00,060
to produce and sell his small moto tool for hobbyists.

293
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,060
Marketed as a hundred tools in one,

294
00:22:03,500 --> 00:22:07,480
it would grind, route, and polish.

295
00:22:08,040 --> 00:22:10,240
It's still made in Racine by the Dremel Company

296
00:22:10,241 --> 00:22:15,700
and today performs all those jobs and many others.

297
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:23,240
Companies like Milwaukee, Black & Decker, Porter Cable, and Sears

298
00:22:23,241 --> 00:22:25,560
had no sooner emerged from the Depression

299
00:22:25,561 --> 00:22:28,080
when they were plunged into World War II.

300
00:22:28,840 --> 00:22:31,140
The experience at Porter Cable was typical.

301
00:22:31,720 --> 00:22:33,740
Still yearning to reach a mass market,

302
00:22:34,200 --> 00:22:38,480
their primary mission, now, was meeting the demands of the U.S. military.

303
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:44,540
Porter Cable got into the effort by supplying what was known as the G8 belt sander.

304
00:22:44,960 --> 00:22:48,520
And this was a large floor-standing machine that was used in the manufacturing tank

305
00:22:48,521 --> 00:22:50,800
and heavy armament as well as the aircraft industry.

306
00:22:51,300 --> 00:22:55,260
We were also called into service to make things that we had no knowledge or practicality of,

307
00:22:55,261 --> 00:23:00,700
such as valves for torpedoes, aircraft parts, as well as parachute ripcords.

308
00:23:03,720 --> 00:23:07,660
Meanwhile at Black & Decker, Al Jr. was brought back to the company

309
00:23:07,661 --> 00:23:11,140
and was rising quickly through the executive ranks.

310
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:13,500
He was offered a commission in the Army,

311
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:17,800
but the government declared his work at Black & Decker essential to the war effort.

312
00:23:18,280 --> 00:23:21,880
So Decker stayed and fought the war from the factory floor,

313
00:23:21,881 --> 00:23:26,020
where the demand for electric drills was higher than ever before.

314
00:23:27,100 --> 00:23:32,020
In building airplanes, all the holes were all drilled with electric drills.

315
00:23:32,460 --> 00:23:37,420
So we began getting orders for electric drills that were just out of this world.

316
00:23:38,720 --> 00:23:42,420
During the war, Decker received some information that he would tuck away

317
00:23:42,421 --> 00:23:45,620
for an important innovation once the war was over.

318
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:51,580
When the whistle blew, airplane factory employees frequently took their black and Decker drills

319
00:23:51,581 --> 00:23:53,868
home with them. They weren't stealing.

320
00:23:53,869 --> 00:23:57,120
It's just that during the war, the government implored

321
00:23:57,121 --> 00:24:01,346
people with a new national logo, fix it yourself.

322
00:24:01,347 --> 00:24:05,400
Plumbers, electricians, and repairmen were away

323
00:24:05,401 --> 00:24:07,649
in the service or in defense jobs.

324
00:24:07,650 --> 00:24:11,420
Department stores and community centers organized special

325
00:24:11,421 --> 00:24:15,020
workshops to teach people how to do it themselves.

326
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:24,860
After World War II, all this influx of soldiers returning from the war, buying these little

327
00:24:24,861 --> 00:24:29,660
houses, they're still working, they don't really have all that much money, and that's

328
00:24:29,661 --> 00:24:31,860
how the do-it-yourself movement sort of got started.

329
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:36,260
These guys were buying these houses, building these houses, and they were fixing them up

330
00:24:36,261 --> 00:24:36,961
themselves.

331
00:24:36,962 --> 00:24:48,080
After the war, Decker already knew, based on his experience with the airline workers taking drills home, that there was a market for consumer-oriented power tools.

332
00:24:49,360 --> 00:24:53,760
In 1946, Black and Decker introduced just such a line.

333
00:24:54,500 --> 00:25:02,800
They were crafted from lighter weight metals, were smaller, easier to grasp, and had lower amperage than the tools that had built the war machine.

334
00:25:03,760 --> 00:25:05,720
They didn't have to be of the same quality,

335
00:25:06,220 --> 00:25:09,580
because instead of drilling hundreds of holes daily for airplanes,

336
00:25:10,120 --> 00:25:12,760
these were used for weekend household chores.

337
00:25:13,560 --> 00:25:18,920
The compromise in size and power also made the tools affordable for consumers.

338
00:25:20,120 --> 00:25:24,500
Within three years, the company sold over one million quarter-inch drills.

339
00:25:26,720 --> 00:25:30,940
It was much more successful than we ever dreamed that it was going to be.

340
00:25:30,941 --> 00:25:39,600
And we followed up with a very expanded line of products for all of those people who do it themselves.

341
00:25:40,240 --> 00:25:43,860
And it's been an excellent market, and still is.

342
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:50,000
1946 would also see the introduction of another popular power tool.

343
00:25:50,860 --> 00:25:56,060
A Swiss employee of what is now the Skill Bosch Corporation invented this tool.

344
00:25:56,440 --> 00:26:00,780
A handheld version of the scroll saw, the jigsaw.

345
00:26:00,940 --> 00:26:03,820
But it didn't look like this at the time.

346
00:26:05,360 --> 00:26:08,880
Albert Kaufman was attempting to repair his wife's sewing machine.

347
00:26:09,700 --> 00:26:12,780
While working on the machine, my guess, as he was in the garage,

348
00:26:13,400 --> 00:26:14,720
Kaufman had an idea.

349
00:26:15,600 --> 00:26:19,860
He would replace the up-and-down reciprocating stitch rig with a blade.

350
00:26:20,840 --> 00:26:25,200
The result was a portable saw capable of making intricate cuts in wood.

351
00:26:25,201 --> 00:26:34,320
Yet, of all the developments that came out of the post-war years, the most significant

352
00:26:34,321 --> 00:26:38,640
was a new material that would revolutionize power tools.

353
00:26:38,641 --> 00:26:44,000
It would not only make them lighter and less expensive, it would help prevent millions

354
00:26:44,001 --> 00:26:49,780
from having a shocking experience.

355
00:26:49,781 --> 00:26:55,020
In addition to introducing several first-of-their-kind power tools, Porter Cable was unique in the

356
00:26:55,021 --> 00:27:01,740
1930s for offering employee benefits, including stock ownership and an on-site bowling alley.

357
00:27:02,500 --> 00:27:05,340
The Toolbench Power Tools will return.

358
00:27:07,480 --> 00:27:09,807
Electricity and I have a thing.

359
00:27:09,808 --> 00:27:14,160
I've gotten shocked more than I care to admit because I've

360
00:27:14,161 --> 00:27:15,480
tried to do things the wrong way.

361
00:27:15,481 --> 00:27:18,000
If you're going to work on electrical things, stay out of water

362
00:27:18,001 --> 00:27:20,466
and shut the circuit breaker off.

363
00:27:20,467 --> 00:27:24,800
Even if you heeded Tim Allen's advice, prior to the 1960s,

364
00:27:24,801 --> 00:27:27,900
a power tool might have given you a nasty shock anyway.

365
00:27:29,260 --> 00:27:32,120
Back then, the only protection between the user

366
00:27:32,121 --> 00:27:35,400
and an electrical shock was the grounding wire

367
00:27:35,401 --> 00:27:38,560
that ran through the third prong of a power cord.

368
00:27:44,200 --> 00:27:47,060
Plastic would become the key to making power tools

369
00:27:47,061 --> 00:27:50,440
not only safer, but lighter and more durable.

370
00:27:51,340 --> 00:27:57,880
However, when plastic first replaced metal on power tools in the 1960s, it left many suspicious.

371
00:27:58,840 --> 00:28:05,260
People associated plastic with the terrible stench that arose when a toy fell behind a radiator and melted.

372
00:28:06,720 --> 00:28:09,820
That was the image that plastic had in that time frame.

373
00:28:09,821 --> 00:28:11,760
We weren't using those kinds of plastics.

374
00:28:11,761 --> 00:28:18,140
And our pitch to our customers was that if you ride in an airplane,

375
00:28:18,480 --> 00:28:22,260
quite probably the windows are made out of the same material as our drills are made out of.

376
00:28:23,060 --> 00:28:29,480
The plastic used on both airplane windows and power tool housings is a polycarbonate resin,

377
00:28:30,060 --> 00:28:33,460
which is much stronger than the softer plastic used on toys.

378
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:38,960
Plastic manufacturers proved the strength of this material in a convincing way.

379
00:28:38,961 --> 00:28:42,040
They fired a .357 Magnum at it.

380
00:28:42,620 --> 00:28:44,720
The bullet couldn't penetrate the plastic.

381
00:28:47,400 --> 00:28:53,180
But an even more important reason for using plastic was that it made power tools a lot safer.

382
00:28:56,460 --> 00:29:03,460
In the 1960s, new methods of machining plastic resins made it possible to double insulate tools.

383
00:29:04,180 --> 00:29:09,340
Now, besides the grounding wire, another layer of protection would be added to the motor.

384
00:29:10,300 --> 00:29:14,660
Right at the heart of our motors is the rotor, or what we call the armature,

385
00:29:15,540 --> 00:29:18,320
which is the moving part of the electric motor.

386
00:29:18,740 --> 00:29:26,660
A single insulated power tool would have pressed this lamination directly on the shaft, steel to steel.

387
00:29:28,300 --> 00:29:33,440
The problem with steel to steel contact is that if a short occurred inside the motor,

388
00:29:33,460 --> 00:29:35,279
it would shock the user.

389
00:29:35,280 --> 00:29:40,280
In a double insulated motor, the steel parts are separated by this

390
00:29:40,281 --> 00:29:46,989
white protective plastic resin. In its raw form, the resin looks like putty.

391
00:29:46,990 --> 00:29:47,960
It can even

392
00:29:47,961 --> 00:29:54,424
be rolled and molded like putty. Here, it's placed into a pressure heating machine.

393
00:29:54,425 --> 00:29:55,125
It

394
00:29:55,126 --> 00:29:59,680
will actually be baked onto motors, which in this case are for craftsmen's circular saws.

395
00:30:03,460 --> 00:30:12,300
This all takes place at the Ryobi Power Tool Plant in Pickens, South Carolina.

396
00:30:12,820 --> 00:30:17,420
But similar procedures are used by most power tool manufacturers today.

397
00:30:19,800 --> 00:30:25,700
And even as these double-insulated saws wind their way through the rest of the assembly process,

398
00:30:26,100 --> 00:30:29,600
they won't reach consumers until they're proven safe.

399
00:30:33,460 --> 00:30:37,920
Each tool is individually tested before going into its box.

400
00:30:40,480 --> 00:30:47,260
Samples of each different tool manufactured at Ryobi are pulled off the assembly line for even further testing.

401
00:30:49,580 --> 00:30:52,080
What happens to this sander is typical.

402
00:30:52,580 --> 00:30:55,160
This is called the six-foot drop test.

403
00:30:55,161 --> 00:31:05,100
Now the point of this test isn't to make sure the tool doesn't break when it's dropped

404
00:31:05,101 --> 00:31:09,520
More importantly, it's to make sure that no damage has occurred in the motor

405
00:31:09,521 --> 00:31:12,160
That might lead to an electrical malfunction

406
00:31:12,161 --> 00:31:14,120
That's the force

407
00:31:14,121 --> 00:31:16,700
1170 pounds

408
00:31:16,701 --> 00:31:21,060
That that sander hit with

409
00:31:21,061 --> 00:31:27,440
After the test, the tool is run on diagnostic equipment to assure its safety.

410
00:31:29,120 --> 00:31:32,640
Other problems could be detected by the sound the tool makes.

411
00:31:33,460 --> 00:31:37,920
Once the tester sets up a microphone, the sander is ready for a solo.

412
00:31:37,921 --> 00:31:51,921
Any unusual changes in the tone and pitch of the tool would indicate an operational problem

413
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:55,349
that would have to be further investigated.

414
00:31:55,350 --> 00:31:59,480
You can't enter this room without heavy ear protection.

415
00:31:59,481 --> 00:32:07,940
A dozen or more power tools run continuously, sometimes for weeks at a time.

416
00:32:09,040 --> 00:32:14,740
The goal was to figure out how long each tool can run before having any motor problems.

417
00:32:18,700 --> 00:32:20,320
Ah, that's better.

418
00:32:21,140 --> 00:32:25,700
While plastic and such extensive testing made power tools better and safer,

419
00:32:26,200 --> 00:32:28,840
another great advance made them easier to use.

420
00:32:28,841 --> 00:32:32,180
It occurred when power tools shed their cords.

421
00:32:36,140 --> 00:32:39,960
Beginning in World War II, battery technology was advancing.

422
00:32:40,760 --> 00:32:47,260
Some of the very first rechargeable batteries were used to trigger torpedoes from submarines and battleships.

423
00:32:47,920 --> 00:32:54,060
I had been reading about these for quite some time, and the improvement in the batteries was so substantial.

424
00:32:54,061 --> 00:33:01,600
I kept wondering if we couldn't possibly make such an improvement in a power drill that could make use of batteries.

425
00:33:02,780 --> 00:33:09,960
In 1961, while many companies were exploring the possibility of introducing battery-operated power tools,

426
00:33:10,660 --> 00:33:15,800
Black & Decker is credited with bringing the first cordless tool, a drill, to market.

427
00:33:16,840 --> 00:33:22,940
It was powered by rechargeable nickel-cadmium batteries, developed during World War II.

428
00:33:24,060 --> 00:33:27,880
The cordless drill sparked a revolution in the industry.

429
00:33:28,200 --> 00:33:34,040
It freed the user from being tied to a wall socket, but also had some disadvantages.

430
00:33:35,040 --> 00:33:41,880
The 4.8-volt batteries were not powerful enough for heavy-duty work and required overnight charging.

431
00:33:42,780 --> 00:33:47,120
It wouldn't be until the 1980s that these problems were solved.

432
00:33:47,740 --> 00:33:52,940
But first, in 1971, cordless technology, limited as it might be,

433
00:33:53,320 --> 00:33:55,860
would play a key role in space exploration.

434
00:33:57,060 --> 00:34:00,940
Astronauts Dave Scott and Jim Irwin took a cordless drill to the moon

435
00:34:00,941 --> 00:34:03,500
as part of their Apollo 15 mission.

436
00:34:04,300 --> 00:34:10,480
There, they would experience both the joys and the frustrations of early cordless tools.

437
00:34:13,560 --> 00:34:16,100
Using the drill to probe the lunar surface,

438
00:34:16,101 --> 00:34:19,120
they would conduct an extensive geological survey.

439
00:34:21,100 --> 00:34:24,720
While the drill's bits and battery pack were made by NASA,

440
00:34:25,220 --> 00:34:27,580
the powerhead was made by Black & Decker.

441
00:34:28,360 --> 00:34:31,400
The powerhead consists of the motor and the chuck,

442
00:34:31,660 --> 00:34:33,820
which is the device that grips the drill bit.

443
00:34:35,200 --> 00:34:37,680
Scott used the drill to acquire rock samples

444
00:34:37,681 --> 00:34:41,260
and to sink a heat-measuring probe deep into the lunar surface.

445
00:34:41,261 --> 00:34:47,660
But the drill, only about half as powerful as most of today's consumer drills,

446
00:34:48,320 --> 00:34:50,860
strained to bore through the soil and rock.

447
00:34:51,780 --> 00:34:56,600
In fact, Dave Scott would have bruised fingernails for several weeks after the mission

448
00:34:56,601 --> 00:34:59,320
because of the pressure he had to exert.

449
00:35:02,060 --> 00:35:05,160
Okay, Dave, take heart. You've got just one minute of drilling left.

450
00:35:05,620 --> 00:35:07,340
Okay, we made a little bit of effort.

451
00:35:07,341 --> 00:35:14,360
In spite of the strain, the drill succeeded in helping to retrieve samples from eight feet beneath the lunar surface.

452
00:35:15,360 --> 00:35:18,740
Five years later, America would celebrate its bicentennial.

453
00:35:18,880 --> 00:35:26,280
This year-long birthday party not only honored America, it sparked another great era of do-it-yourself.

454
00:35:26,960 --> 00:35:32,680
Among other things, the celebration would inspire Americans to refurbish historical sites.

455
00:35:32,681 --> 00:35:39,420
The effort would revitalize the do-it-yourself movement that was so prevalent in the years after World War II.

456
00:35:40,100 --> 00:35:42,860
It's time to talk some of my favorite work, which is the sidewalk.

457
00:35:43,220 --> 00:35:49,080
Perhaps nobody received more attention for their efforts than a Cuban-born architect named Bob Vila.

458
00:35:49,540 --> 00:35:54,840
The bicentennial in this country really got folks excited about polishing the brass doorknob,

459
00:35:55,460 --> 00:36:00,580
about cleaning the windows, about looking at their 100-year-old house and saying,

460
00:36:00,581 --> 00:36:02,380
this is a valuable piece of history.

461
00:36:02,760 --> 00:36:04,980
I gotta, you know, I gotta fix it up.

462
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:08,800
Vila was involved in restoring a Victorian home

463
00:36:08,801 --> 00:36:09,880
that was so impressive,

464
00:36:10,400 --> 00:36:12,520
it garnered national media coverage.

465
00:36:14,140 --> 00:36:17,440
Vila's uncanny ability to explain his art and technique

466
00:36:17,441 --> 00:36:19,580
in an interesting and informative way

467
00:36:19,581 --> 00:36:22,220
landed him his first television series,

468
00:36:22,740 --> 00:36:25,020
This Old House, in 1978.

469
00:36:25,640 --> 00:36:29,940
With the realities of the economy in the 70s and in the 80s,

470
00:36:29,941 --> 00:36:37,880
where a lot of people simply didn't have the money to hire craftsmen and artisans to come and do the paint job

471
00:36:37,881 --> 00:36:42,180
or do certain repair work for them that they had to do it themselves.

472
00:36:42,380 --> 00:36:47,140
And when we came along with the home improvement shows, we were kind of empowering them.

473
00:36:48,200 --> 00:36:54,080
Over the next 20 years, Vila would go on to host other do-it-yourself series, including Home Again,

474
00:36:54,360 --> 00:36:58,900
and become the standard for at least a half dozen similar series to follow.

475
00:36:59,940 --> 00:37:07,420
By the early 1980s, Vila's wide audience of do-it-yourselfers were in the market for newer, better power tools.

476
00:37:08,380 --> 00:37:14,220
In response, several power tool manufacturers introduced new batteries that were not only more powerful,

477
00:37:14,840 --> 00:37:19,140
but were interchangeable between tools like drills, sanders, and jigsaws.

478
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:26,900
Now the industry was producing easier to use, safer, and more versatile cordless tools than ever before.

479
00:37:27,520 --> 00:37:33,940
Yet, the thirst for more power was about to push power tools to yet another plateau.

480
00:37:36,960 --> 00:37:44,940
The battery was invented in 1800 by Italian physicist Alessandro Volta, for whom the Volt is named.

481
00:37:45,580 --> 00:37:47,380
Modern marvels will return.

482
00:37:52,020 --> 00:37:52,970
Well, let's face it.

483
00:37:52,971 --> 00:37:56,200
We can come up with all sorts of good reasons for using power tools.

484
00:37:56,201 --> 00:37:59,240
We save money by doing home improvement jobs ourselves.

485
00:37:59,420 --> 00:38:01,640
We can work more quickly and more efficiently.

486
00:38:02,040 --> 00:38:05,040
We can build little birdhouses for our feathered friends.

487
00:38:05,440 --> 00:38:06,140
But you know what?

488
00:38:06,420 --> 00:38:10,260
The truth is, most people just think more power is really cool.

489
00:38:14,340 --> 00:38:25,700
On almost any given weekend, cities across the country play host to do-it-yourself fairs.

490
00:38:25,701 --> 00:38:28,220
like this one outside Cleveland, Ohio.

491
00:38:29,200 --> 00:38:32,760
Here, do-it-yourself gurus come to inspire the crowds.

492
00:38:33,660 --> 00:38:39,480
It's also a chance for toolmakers to show off the powerful capabilities of their newest tools.

493
00:38:40,220 --> 00:38:41,620
Fantastic machine.

494
00:38:42,160 --> 00:38:47,500
Remember Albert Kaufman who converted his wife's sewing machine to a jigsaw for the Bosch company?

495
00:38:48,120 --> 00:38:53,040
Well, today, the company is known as Skill Bosch, and it's still making jigsaws.

496
00:38:53,041 --> 00:38:58,540
But now jigsaws can take a power plunge like this

497
00:38:58,541 --> 00:39:01,080
and still make an intricate cut

498
00:39:01,081 --> 00:39:06,360
Remember Art Emmons who invented the takeabout sander in 1926?

499
00:39:07,020 --> 00:39:08,840
Well, it's no longer called the takeabout

500
00:39:08,841 --> 00:39:14,840
but Porter Cable's newest belt sander still has Art Emmons' creative fingerprints all over it

501
00:39:14,841 --> 00:39:18,840
including the basics of the design and the dust collector

502
00:39:18,841 --> 00:39:22,980
Remember A.H. Pearson?

503
00:39:23,040 --> 00:39:25,360
who invented the hole shooter for Henry Ford?

504
00:39:26,100 --> 00:39:29,160
Well, now the company that emerged, Milwaukee Tools,

505
00:39:29,640 --> 00:39:31,760
is best known for the Sawzall.

506
00:39:34,160 --> 00:39:37,020
You take the Sawzall, you push it against the material you're cutting

507
00:39:37,021 --> 00:39:38,300
with the light pressure on the top.

508
00:39:38,580 --> 00:39:41,740
You should never have to overload the tool at all, but the tool did the work.

509
00:39:42,660 --> 00:39:47,120
Contractors have loved this tool since it was first introduced in the 1950s

510
00:39:47,121 --> 00:39:51,120
because it cuts through darn near anything at all sorts of angles.

511
00:39:53,040 --> 00:40:00,040
Meanwhile, the innovator of the pistol grip drill, Black & Decker, offers a cordless power

512
00:40:00,041 --> 00:40:03,960
tool line with batteries that exceed 14 volts.

513
00:40:03,961 --> 00:40:08,540
Throughout most of the 90s, this much power was reserved for the professionals.

514
00:40:08,541 --> 00:40:12,640
Now it's marketed to consumers.

515
00:40:12,641 --> 00:40:18,260
Instead of the traditional nickel-cadmium batteries, these tools are powered by nickel-metal hydride,

516
00:40:18,261 --> 00:40:22,240
a chemical compound used for cell phones and laptop computers.

517
00:40:23,440 --> 00:40:28,420
For years, that chemical compound did not fit well with power tools

518
00:40:28,421 --> 00:40:30,940
because although it yielded great run time,

519
00:40:31,260 --> 00:40:33,300
it didn't do a great job from a power standpoint.

520
00:40:33,760 --> 00:40:35,820
But what we did is we worked with our battery supplier,

521
00:40:35,980 --> 00:40:36,920
in this case Panasonic,

522
00:40:37,420 --> 00:40:40,000
and have developed kind of a breakthrough for power tools.

523
00:40:40,380 --> 00:40:44,240
The development of batteries that offer the longevity of a cell phone

524
00:40:44,241 --> 00:40:48,000
with the power to spin this 24-volt circular saw

525
00:40:48,001 --> 00:40:52,400
seems an appropriate way to end 100 years of power tool history.

526
00:40:53,060 --> 00:40:54,060
But not quite.

527
00:40:54,620 --> 00:40:58,860
Because a new name, one associated with not only tools, but laughter,

528
00:40:59,380 --> 00:41:02,260
has joined the fraternity of power tool manufacturers.

529
00:41:03,740 --> 00:41:05,780
It's a cutthroat business, this tool business.

530
00:41:06,000 --> 00:41:08,480
I think they all get along, but I guess it's just like the other,

531
00:41:08,580 --> 00:41:11,140
Stanley and Black & Decker and all the companies.

532
00:41:11,141 --> 00:41:13,780
They're very competitive, and now all of a sudden we're a player.

533
00:41:14,320 --> 00:41:17,720
Tim Allen is still an actor, as evidenced by the action hero makeup

534
00:41:17,721 --> 00:41:19,580
he's wearing on this film set.

535
00:41:20,060 --> 00:41:23,280
However, he isn't acting anymore when it comes to tools.

536
00:41:24,020 --> 00:41:27,680
In 1999, Alan launched his own line of power tools.

537
00:41:28,140 --> 00:41:29,260
They include a jigsaw,

538
00:41:31,160 --> 00:41:31,860
sander,

539
00:41:32,980 --> 00:41:34,060
and cordless drill.

540
00:41:34,540 --> 00:41:36,760
His inspiration for launching the line?

541
00:41:37,320 --> 00:41:38,240
Salad dressing.

542
00:41:38,880 --> 00:41:42,540
Alan is using his passion for tools to raise money for charity,

543
00:41:42,900 --> 00:41:46,420
just as actor Paul Newman has done with his food products.

544
00:41:47,120 --> 00:41:50,960
You look up to this guy because he's done so much in his career that I would certainly aspire to.

545
00:41:51,680 --> 00:41:54,380
And I was curious one day about why he got into that.

546
00:41:54,381 --> 00:42:01,900
I had the same sensibilities that it's a great way to employ even more people, get people active in charity work.

547
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:07,880
Alan learned about motors and bearings by refurbishing abandoned washing machines as a teenager.

548
00:42:08,520 --> 00:42:13,800
He also used his own background and design to give the tools a personal touch.

549
00:42:14,200 --> 00:42:16,200
I didn't want to just sign my name on something.

550
00:42:16,420 --> 00:42:19,560
I wanted to have some input in how the thing was designed, because I love design.

551
00:42:19,660 --> 00:42:21,480
I love the whole art of design.

552
00:42:21,760 --> 00:42:22,920
I love the science of design.

553
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:25,060
Some of my favorite heroes are designers.

554
00:42:25,500 --> 00:42:27,280
So I wanted to be able to get involved in that process.

555
00:42:27,920 --> 00:42:33,120
Alan worked with a design team from the Ryobi Corporation, a toolmaker that manufactures the line.

556
00:42:33,960 --> 00:42:39,100
His power tools reflect many of the changes the industry has made in the last decade

557
00:42:39,101 --> 00:42:43,040
to make power tools more user-friendly than ever before.

558
00:42:43,041 --> 00:42:47,000
A slot holds extra drill bits

559
00:42:47,001 --> 00:42:49,600
The grip is ergonomically designed

560
00:42:49,601 --> 00:42:54,120
Meaning it's made to feel comfortable and ballast in the user's hand

561
00:42:54,121 --> 00:42:58,700
And a bubble level helps identify a perfect 90 degree angle

562
00:42:58,701 --> 00:43:03,780
Such concerns for convenience would probably seem a bit arcane

563
00:43:03,781 --> 00:43:08,000
To the men who invented the first electric drill a hundred years earlier

564
00:43:08,001 --> 00:43:11,040
Or to Alonzo Decker and Duncan Black

565
00:43:11,041 --> 00:43:14,420
who designed the pistol grip drill a short time later.

566
00:43:15,100 --> 00:43:19,080
But they also carry on the spirit of these early innovators,

567
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:25,320
whose goals included making our lives a bit easier and much more productive.

568
00:43:29,920 --> 00:43:33,720
You know, I'm really struck by how good-looking power tools are today.

569
00:43:34,080 --> 00:43:38,220
I could use this to drill a hole, or I could put it on my fireplace as a work of art.

570
00:43:38,221 --> 00:43:42,220
But to the old timers, a power tool was for nothing but work.

571
00:43:42,221 --> 00:43:46,220
About 50 years ago, a friend of Porter cable engineer Art Emmons

572
00:43:46,221 --> 00:43:51,220
took his dull metal take about Sander, like this one, had it polished to a beautiful shine,

573
00:43:51,221 --> 00:43:53,220
and presented it to Art as a gift.

574
00:43:53,221 --> 00:43:56,220
Emmons took one look at it, shook his head and said,

575
00:43:56,221 --> 00:43:58,220
You ruined it!

576
00:43:58,221 --> 00:44:02,220
From Modern Marvels, I'm Ron Hazleton.

577
00:44:08,220 --> 00:44:10,280
you

